insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-seismic-and-the-well-tie', 1, 'A seismic trace plots recorded amplitude against which independent variable?', '["Measured depth below the rotary table","One-way travel time from the reflector to the surface","Acoustic impedance","Two-way travel time"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The receiver clock starts when the source fires and records energy that has travelled down to a reflector and back up again, so a trace is indexed in two-way travel time, in milliseconds rather than metres.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-seismic-and-the-well-tie', 2, 'A 60 m thick, acoustically uniform sand sits between shales of different impedance. How many reflection events does the reflectivity series contain across the sand?', '["None, because uniform rock does not reflect at any of its boundaries","One, at the middle of the sand where the body is thickest","Two, one at the top interface and one at the base interface","One per sample through the sand, because each sample has a property value"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Reflections come from contrasts in acoustic impedance, not from rock properties themselves. Nothing changes inside the sand so its interior is acoustically silent, and only the top and base interfaces produce spikes.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-seismic-and-the-well-tie', 3, 'Which statement best expresses the convolutional model as the course states it?', '["A seismic trace is approximately the earth''s reflectivity series convolved with a source wavelet","A seismic trace is the acoustic impedance log resampled into time","A seismic trace is the sum of the velocity and density logs at each interface","A seismic trace is the source wavelet divided by the earth''s reflectivity series"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The reflectivity series is the earth''s contribution and the wavelet is the acquisition''s contribution. Convolution replaces every reflectivity spike with a scaled copy of the wavelet and adds the overlaps.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-seismic-and-the-well-tie', 4, 'How many depth samples does basic_20.las contain?', '["150","300","301","302"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The log runs from 1500 to 1650 m at a 0.5 m step. That is 300 steps, and because both endpoints are sampled the count is 301.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-seismic-and-the-well-tie', 5, 'Which two curves in basic_20.las are the ones a synthetic seismogram is built from?', '["GR and NPHI","DEPT and GR","NPHI and RHOB","DT and RHOB"]'::jsonb, 3, 'DT is inverted to velocity and RHOB supplies density; their product is acoustic impedance. GR and NPHI are petrophysical curves and take no part in the synthetic.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-seismic-and-the-well-tie', 6, 'What is the declared null value in basic_20.las?', '["-999.25","-9999","0","999.25"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The file declares -999.25, the common LAS sentinel. Nulls must be excluded before any statistic is computed, or the sentinel contaminates the average.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-seismic-and-the-well-tie', 7, 'In basic_20.las, DT is 399.737 us/m at 1500 m and 277.473 us/m at 1650 m. What does that trend indicate?', '["The rock transmits sound faster at the base of the log than at the top","The rock transmits sound more slowly at the base of the log than at the top","Velocity is constant because DT is a slowness rather than a velocity","Density must be falling downward to compensate"]'::jsonb, 0, 'DT is interval transit time, a slowness, so velocity is its reciprocal. A falling DT means a rising velocity, which together with the rising RHOB is the normal compaction pattern.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-seismic-and-the-well-tie', 8, 'The mean sonic velocity of the teaching well is 3145.29 m/s. How is that value defined?', '["The reciprocal of the mean DT over the log","The average of the velocities at the top and base of the log only","The velocity of the 2000 m/s teaching overburden scaled by density","The mean of the velocities of all 301 finite DT samples"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Velocity is computed sample by sample and then averaged over all 301 finite DT samples. Averaging DT first and inverting gives a different number, because reciprocals do not preserve averages.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-seismic-and-the-well-tie', 9, 'The course uses a teaching time-depth function based on a vertical well with a 2000 m/s overburden. What does it produce?', '["Two-way time in milliseconds numerically equal to depth in metres","Two-way time in seconds equal to depth in metres","Two-way time in milliseconds equal to half the depth in metres","Two-way time in milliseconds equal to twice the depth in metres"]'::jsonb, 0, 'With TWT(z) = 2z/2000 seconds, a depth of 1500 m maps to 1500 ms and 1650 m maps to 1650 ms, so the numbers are deliberately easy to check by eye.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-seismic-and-the-well-tie', 10, 'At what two-way time does the top of the teaching log sit under the course time-depth function?', '["750 ms","1500 ms","1650 ms","3000 ms"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The log top is at 1500 m, and the teaching function makes two-way time in milliseconds numerically equal to depth in metres, so the top sits at 1500 ms.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-seismic-and-the-well-tie', 11, 'Which value is the maximum acoustic impedance graded in the capstone?', '["5476.85","8189.64","10624.96","3145.29"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The maximum is 10624.96. Note that it is not the base-of-log value of 8189.64, because impedance rises overall but not monotonically and the peak sits inside the interval.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-seismic-and-the-well-tie', 12, 'What is the strongest reflection coefficient in the teaching well, and what is its sign?', '["0.073005, positive","0.017688, positive","0.3, negative","0.017688, negative"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The strongest coefficient is 0.017688 in absolute value and it is negative, meaning acoustic impedance drops downward across that interface.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-seismic-and-the-well-tie', 13, 'The strongest reflection coefficient sits at 1582 ms but the strongest amplitude on the 25 Hz synthetic sits at 1642 ms. What explains the difference?', '["The time-depth function shifts deeper events by about 60 ms","Several moderate coefficients near 1642 ms interfere constructively once convolved with the wavelet","The reflection coefficient at 1582 ms is negative, so it is discarded from the synthetic","Density was not applied at 1582 ms, which suppressed that event"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Convolution replaces each spike with a wavelet copy and overlapping copies add. Reinforcement between several ordinary interfaces near 1642 ms beats the single isolated spike at 1582 ms, which is why a bright event does not imply a large contrast.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-seismic-and-the-well-tie', 14, 'In which order does the course build the synthetic pipeline?', '["Reflection coefficients, then impedance, then convolution, then the time axis","Impedance, then the time axis, then reflection coefficients, then convolution","The time axis, then convolution, then impedance, then reflection coefficients","Convolution, then impedance, then reflection coefficients, then the time axis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Module 2 builds impedance from DT and RHOB, module 3 converts depth to two-way time, module 4 differences impedance into reflection coefficients, and module 5 convolves with a Ricker wavelet before module 6 reads the panel.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-seismic-and-the-well-tie', 15, 'Why does the course insist that the tie, not the seismic alone, is what lets a horizon be mapped honestly?', '["Seismic amplitudes are always too weak to pick without a well to boost them","Only the tie establishes which seismic reflection corresponds to which geological surface","Seismic surveys record depth directly, so the tie merely confirms the depths","Horizon mapping is done in the well domain, so seismic contributes nothing to it"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The tie answers one question with evidence: which reflection carries which surface. Picking the visually strongest event instead can produce a fully self-consistent map of the wrong surface, which is harder to catch than having no tie at all.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-velocity-and-impedance', 1, 'What physical quantity does the sonic log (DT) record directly?', '["Compressional velocity in metres per second","Interval transit time, or slowness, the time a wave takes to cross a fixed distance of formation","Bulk density of the formation including its pore fluids","Acoustic impedance, the product of density and velocity"]'::jsonb, 1, 'No logging tool writes a velocity curve directly. The sonic tool records interval transit time, conventionally called DT, and the property it carries is slowness. Velocity is derived from it by taking the reciprocal.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-velocity-and-impedance', 2, 'A rock interval shows a high deflection on the DT track. What does that tell you about the rock?', '["It is a fast rock, because high transit time means high velocity","It tells you nothing about velocity without the density curve","It is a slow rock, because high transit time means low velocity","It is a dense rock, because DT is a density measurement"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Slowness is the reciprocal of velocity. A rock that transmits sound slowly takes a long time to cross each metre, so it has a high transit time and a low velocity. High on the DT track means slow.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-velocity-and-impedance', 3, 'The teaching well records DT in microseconds per metre. Which expression converts it to velocity in metres per second?', '["$v = 10^{6} / \\Delta t$","$v = \\Delta t / 10^{6}$","$v = \\Delta t \\times 10^{6}$","$v = 10^{6} \\times \\Delta t^{2}$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A transit time of dt microseconds per metre is dt times 10^-6 seconds per metre. Inverting that gives metres per second, so v = 1000000 / dt. The engine function slownessToVelocity implements exactly this.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-velocity-and-impedance', 4, 'At 1500 m the teaching well has DT of 399.737 us/m. What is the velocity?', '["0.0025 m/s","399737000 m/s","3997.37 m/s","2501.65 m/s"]'::jsonb, 3, '1000000 / 399.737 gives 2501.65 m/s. Dropping the factor of 10^6 gives 0.0025, and multiplying by 10^6 instead of dividing gives 399737000. Both are obvious order-of-magnitude errors once you know that clastic velocities at this depth are in the low thousands of metres per second.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-velocity-and-impedance', 5, 'At 1650 m the teaching well has DT of 277.473 us/m. What is the velocity?', '["3603.96 m/s","277473000 m/s","0.0036 m/s","2774.73 m/s"]'::jsonb, 0, '1000000 / 277.473 gives 3603.96 m/s. Note that this is the shortest transit time of the anchor depths and therefore the highest velocity, which is the inverse relationship at work.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-velocity-and-impedance', 6, 'Applying the conversion to all 301 samples of the teaching log and averaging the finite results gives what mean velocity?', '["2501.65 m/s","3603.96 m/s","3145.29 m/s","5476.85 m/s"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The whole-log mean velocity is 3145.29 m/s, one of the six values the capstone grades. It is the simple arithmetic mean over finite samples, with nulls excluded rather than counted as zero, and it is not the time-weighted average that the depth-to-time module introduces.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-velocity-and-impedance', 7, 'You receive a well whose sonic curve is recorded in microseconds per foot. What must happen before it reaches the velocity step?', '["Nothing, because the reciprocal formula is unit independent","The curve must be converted, because feeding a foot-based DT into the metre formula yields a velocity in the wrong units","The density curve must be converted instead, since the sonic is always correct","The depth column must be renamed, which is enough to fix the units"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The conversion v = 1000000 / dt is correct only for microseconds per metre. A foot-based DT put through it produces a plausible-looking number in the wrong units, and everything downstream assumes metres per second. Read the curve unit string in the header before converting, as the Well Data course covered.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-velocity-and-impedance', 8, 'What does the RHOB curve measure?', '["The grain density of the rock matrix, with pore fluids excluded","The density of the pore fluid alone","The porosity of the formation as a volume fraction","Bulk density, the mass of a unit volume of rock including its pore fluids"]'::jsonb, 3, 'RHOB is bulk density in grams per cubic centimetre. It measures the formation as it sits in the ground, grains and pore fluids together, which is what a passing seismic wave has to move.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-velocity-and-impedance', 9, 'Which of these is one of the three controls on bulk density named in the lesson?', '["Porosity, because pore space replaces heavier mineral with lighter fluid","The seismic sample rate used for the survey","The wavelet frequency at the reservoir level","The two-way time to the top of the interval"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The three controls are matrix mineralogy, porosity and fluid fill. Porosity pulls bulk density down because every unit of pore space is a unit of mineral replaced by a lighter fluid, which is why RHOB is the workhorse porosity log.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-velocity-and-impedance', 10, 'Why does the synthetic workflow need density and not velocity alone?', '["Because density is measured more accurately than velocity in every well","Because reflections respond to impedance, which is the product of the two, so a velocity increase can be offset by a density decrease","Because density is required to convert depth into two-way time","Because velocity cannot be computed without a density curve to calibrate it"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Impedance is a product, so a rise in one factor can be cancelled by a fall in the other. A boundary where velocity increases and density decreases by a comparable proportion produces little impedance contrast and therefore little reflection, even though the sonic log shows a clear step.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-velocity-and-impedance', 11, 'Acoustic impedance is defined as which of the following?', '["The difference between velocity and density","The ratio of velocity to density","The product of density and velocity, $Z = \\rho v$","The reciprocal of velocity multiplied by porosity"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Z equals rho times v. Physically it measures how strongly a material resists being moved by a passing wave: stiff and heavy together means hard to disturb, which is what high impedance describes.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-velocity-and-impedance', 12, 'At 1580 m the velocity is 3277.17 m/s and RHOB is 2.4349 g/cc. What is the acoustic impedance in course units?', '["1345.92","7979.58","3279.60","5476.85"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Multiplying gives 7979.58 in units of (m/s) times (g/cc). Dividing instead of multiplying gives 1345.92 and adding gives 3279.60, while 5476.85 is the impedance at 1500 m rather than at 1580 m.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-velocity-and-impedance', 13, 'At 1650 m the velocity is 3603.96 m/s and RHOB is 2.2724 g/cc. What is the acoustic impedance in course units?', '["3606.23","1585.97","8189.64","10624.96"]'::jsonb, 2, '3603.96 times 2.2724 gives 8189.64. Note that between 1580 m and 1650 m velocity rose while density fell, so the two effects partly cancelled and impedance rose only modestly from 7979.58 to 8189.64.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-velocity-and-impedance', 14, 'Across the teaching log, what is the maximum acoustic impedance in course units?', '["10624.96","5436.47","8189.64","3145.29"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Impedance runs from a minimum of 5436.47 to a maximum of 10624.96, and the maximum is one of the six values the capstone grades. The maximum is just under twice the minimum.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-velocity-and-impedance', 15, 'A null appears in the RHOB curve at one depth while the DT curve is valid there. What does the engine write into the impedance log at that depth?', '["The velocity value on its own, since density is treated as optional per sample","A value interpolated from the two neighbouring impedance samples","Zero, so that the sample count stays complete","A gap, because a null in either input propagates straight into the product"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Impedance is a product and is only as good as both factors. Where either input is a null, non-finite or non-positive, the engine writes a gap and interpolates nothing. An invented value would create an invented contrast, an invented reflection coefficient and a reflector that does not exist in the rock.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-depth-to-time', 1, 'What are the two quantities a seismic receiver actually records for a reflection event?', '["The depth of the interface and the velocity of the rock above it","The strength of the returning ground motion and the elapsed time since the shot","The thickness of the reflecting bed and its acoustic impedance","The one-way travel time and the dip of the interface"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A receiver measures ground motion against elapsed time. Depth is never measured directly; it is inferred later, and only once a velocity model exists.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-depth-to-time', 2, 'Why does the industry interpret seismic data in time and convert to depth only at the end?', '["Because time displays are easier to print and archive than depth displays","Because converting to depth needs a velocity model, which is one of the things interpretation is supposed to produce","Because seismic processing cannot output data on a depth axis at all","Because well tops are also recorded in travel time, so both datasets already agree"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Depth conversion requires knowing the velocity of every layer the energy crossed, and that knowledge comes out of the interpretation. Interpreting in time first breaks the circle, and the conversion becomes a deliberate final step.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-depth-to-time', 3, 'Which statement about the asymmetry between the two conversion directions is correct?', '["Both directions carry identical error, since one is just the inverse of the other","Time to depth is the easy direction, because seismic times are measured very precisely","Depth to time is straightforward given a velocity model, while time to depth inherits every error in that model","Neither direction can be done without a checkshot survey in the well"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Going from depth to time you already know where you are and the errors stay bounded. Going the other way, a velocity error is multiplied by travel time and becomes a systematic depth error across the whole map.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-depth-to-time', 4, 'A reflector sits at 1500 m with an average overburden velocity of 2000 m/s. What is its two-way time?', '["750 ms","1500 ms","3000 ms","0.75 ms"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The path length is 2 times 1500, which is 3000 m. Dividing by 2000 m/s gives 1.5 s, which is 1500 ms. The 750 ms option is the one-way time, produced by forgetting the factor of two.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-depth-to-time', 5, 'Why does the two-way time formula carry a factor of two in the numerator?', '["Because the source emits two pulses for every shot recorded","Because seismic amplitude is recorded as a peak-to-trough pair","Because the sample interval of 2 ms has to be accounted for in the arithmetic","Because the source and receiver are on the same side of the target, so the energy travels down and back"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Reflection seismic has no instrument waiting at the reservoir. The only energy that returns has crossed the overburden twice, so the path length is 2z rather than z.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-depth-to-time', 6, 'You are handed a two-way time of 2400 ms and an average overburden velocity of 2000 m/s. What depth does it correspond to?', '["1200 m","2400 m","4800 m","1.2 m"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Halve the two-way time to get 1200 ms of one-way time, which is 1.2 s, then multiply by 2000 m/s to get 2400 m. Answering 4800 m comes from multiplying the full two-way time by the velocity, which double counts the trip.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-depth-to-time', 7, 'In the sanity check taught in this module, what confirms that the factor of two was handled correctly?', '["Halving the two-way time and multiplying by velocity returns the depth you started with","The two-way time in milliseconds is always larger than the depth in metres","The computed depth falls somewhere inside the 0 to 1798 ms grid","Multiplying the two-way time by velocity returns the depth you started with"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Half the two-way time is the one-way time, and one-way time multiplied by average velocity is depth. Getting back double or half of your starting depth tells you which way you slipped.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-depth-to-time', 8, 'Why do measured depth, true vertical depth and true vertical depth subsea all coincide for the teaching well?', '["Because the sonic log was drift corrected against a checkshot survey","Because all depths were converted to time before being recorded","Because the well is vertical and its reference is placed at mean sea level","Because the logged interval is only 150 m thick, so the differences are negligible"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A vertical hole makes MD equal TVD, and a reference at mean sea level makes TVD equal TVDSS. This is a stated teaching simplification, not a general property of wells.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-depth-to-time', 9, 'Under this course''s teaching time-depth function, what is the relationship between metres of depth and milliseconds of two-way time?', '["The number of milliseconds is twice the number of metres","The number of milliseconds equals the number of metres","The number of milliseconds is half the number of metres","The relationship depends on the local sonic velocity at that depth"]'::jsonb, 1, 'With v fixed at 2000 m/s, TWT(z) = 2z/2000 s = z/1000 s, which is z milliseconds. That identity is why every panel time can be hand checked.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-depth-to-time', 10, 'A reflection appears at 1582 ms. Under the teaching time-depth function, where in the well does it come from?', '["791 m, since the sample index is the depth","3164 m, since the path length is doubled","1582 m, which lies inside the logged interval","It cannot be determined without the mean sonic velocity"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Milliseconds equal metres under the teaching function, so 1582 ms comes from 1582 m. That depth is 82 m below the log top at 1500 m, comfortably inside the 1500 m to 1650 m interval.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-depth-to-time', 11, 'How do the 2000 m/s overburden velocity and the 3145.29 m/s mean sonic velocity differ in role?', '["The 2000 m/s places the log on the time axis, while the 3145.29 m/s is measured rock velocity that builds impedance and reflection coefficients","The 2000 m/s is the measured rock velocity, and the 3145.29 m/s is the assumed overburden average","They are two estimates of the same quantity, and the more precise one should be preferred","The 3145.29 m/s converts depth to time, and the 2000 m/s converts time back to depth"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The 2000 m/s is an assumed overburden average whose only job is positioning on the time axis. The 3145.29 m/s comes from the sonic log and drives impedance and reflectivity, so altering it changes the reflection coefficients.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-depth-to-time', 12, 'How is a real well tie''s time-depth relationship established, rather than by assuming a constant velocity?', '["By assuming the seismic sample interval matches the log sample interval","By reading the two-way time straight off the seismic display at the well location","By averaging stacking velocities from processing over the whole survey","By a checkshot survey or a vertical seismic profile, with the sonic log then drift corrected against it"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A checkshot or VSP measures travel time to known depths in the borehole. The integrated sonic is then drift corrected to agree with those measurements, because sonic readings depart from seismic-scale travel time.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-depth-to-time', 13, 'The time grid holds 900 samples at 2 ms spacing starting at 0 ms. What is the time of the last sample?', '["1798 ms","1800 ms","900 ms","1802 ms"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The last sample is number 899, not 900, because sample 0 occupies the slot at time zero. So the last time is 2 times 899, which is 1798 ms.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-depth-to-time', 14, 'Why must the samples of the synthetic be evenly spaced in time?', '["Because the sonic log is recorded at exactly 2 ms intervals in the borehole","Because discrete convolution requires a constant spacing, and real seismic is recorded that way too","Because uneven spacing would make the well tops land at odd millisecond values","Because the validity mask can only be stored for regularly spaced samples"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Convolution slides one evenly spaced series past another and is undefined without a constant offset. Field data also has a fixed sample interval, so a synthetic on a compatible grid can be compared without interpolation.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-depth-to-time', 15, 'The log occupies 1500 to 1650 ms of a 0 to 1798 ms grid. What does the engine do with the samples outside that window?', '["It fills them with zeros so the trace plots as a continuous line","It extrapolates the nearest log value outward to the ends of the grid","It marks them as gaps and excludes them from every statistic","It shortens the grid so that only the 76 logged samples remain"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Undefined is not zero. Zero filling would claim there is rock with no impedance contrast, manufacture false reflections at the log edges, and let statistics be computed over 824 invented samples, so the engine masks them as gaps instead.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-reflection-coefficients', 1, 'What must differ across a boundary for seismic energy to reflect from it?', '["The age of the two rock units","The thickness of the two rock units","The acoustic impedance of the two rock units","The porosity of the two rock units"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Reflection is generated where acoustic impedance changes across an interface. Age, thickness and porosity matter only insofar as they change velocity and density, and so change impedance.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-reflection-coefficients', 2, 'A layer whose velocity and density are constant throughout is encased in rock of different impedance. What does it contribute to the reflectivity series?', '["Two reflections, one at its top and one at its base, with nothing from its interior","One reflection from the middle of the layer, sized by its thickness","No reflection at all, because uniform layers are acoustically invisible","A continuous train of reflections, one for every sample inside it"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Nothing changes inside the layer, so its interior is silent no matter how thick it is. Impedance changes only where it meets the rock above and the rock below, giving exactly two reflections.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-reflection-coefficients', 3, 'A shale over a sand and a limestone over a dolomite happen to present the same impedance contrast. What does the seismic trace show?', '["Two clearly different events, because the lithologies are different","A reflection only from the shale over sand pair, since clastics reflect more strongly","A reflection only from the limestone over dolomite pair, since carbonates are harder","The same reflection in both cases, because the wave responds to impedance contrast alone"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The wave has no way to report which rocks are involved, only how much their impedances differ. Equal contrasts give equal reflections, which is the fundamental non-uniqueness of seismic interpretation.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-reflection-coefficients', 4, 'An interval on a seismic section shows no reflections. What can you legitimately conclude?', '["The interval is a single uniform rock body with no internal boundaries","The impedances on either side of any boundaries there are too similar to reflect","The interval contains no rock of commercial interest","The seismic data over that interval must be of poor quality"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Absence of an event is evidence that impedances match, not that boundaries are absent. A sharp, mappable geological contact can be seismically invisible when velocity and density multiply out to similar impedance on both sides.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-reflection-coefficients', 5, 'For normal incidence, with layer 1 above and layer 2 below the interface, the reflection coefficient is:', '["(Z2 - Z1) divided by (Z2 times Z1)","(Z2 - Z1) divided by (Z2 + Z1)","(Z1 - Z2) divided by (Z2 + Z1)","(Z2 + Z1) divided by (Z2 - Z1)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The numerator is the downward contrast and the denominator normalises it against the impedance level. Writing the numerator as Z1 minus Z2 reverses the sign convention and would report a soft event as hard.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-reflection-coefficients', 6, 'On the teaching well, impedance is 7979.58 at 1580 m and 7702.20 at 1582 m. What is the reflection coefficient at that interface?', '["-277.38","-0.035376","+0.017688","-0.017688"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The numerator is 7702.20 minus 7979.58, which is -277.38, and the denominator is 7702.20 plus 7979.58, which is 15681.78. Dividing gives -0.017688. The value -277.38 skips the normalisation, -0.035376 divides by the average impedance 7840.89 instead of the sum, and +0.017688 reverses which layer is above.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-reflection-coefficients', 7, 'A learner reports the reflection coefficient between impedances 7979.58 and 7702.20 as -277.38. What did they leave out?', '["They did not divide by the sum of the two impedances, 15681.78","They did not convert the impedances to SI units first","They did not multiply by the sample interval of 2 ms","They did not take the absolute value of the difference"]'::jsonb, 0, '-277.38 is the raw impedance difference. The reflection coefficient normalises that difference by the sum of the two impedances, 15681.78, giving -0.017688. Forgetting the denominator is the most common arithmetic slip in this module.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-reflection-coefficients', 8, 'Which statement about the reflection coefficient is correct?', '["It carries the units of impedance, metres per second times grams per cubic centimetre","It can exceed 1 where the impedance contrast is very large","It is dimensionless and bounded between -1 and +1","It is always positive, because energy cannot be negative"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Numerator and denominator carry the same units so they cancel, making the coefficient a pure ratio. Driving the lower impedance toward zero tends to -1 and toward infinity tends to +1, so the value can never leave that range. Typical sedimentary values are a few hundredths, which is why seismic amplitudes are small numbers.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-reflection-coefficients', 9, 'A reflection coefficient comes out negative. What does that tell you about the rocks?', '["Impedance decreases downward across the interface, a soft event","Impedance increases downward across the interface, a hard event","The interface dips away from the well","The two impedances are equal, so no energy is reflected"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The denominator is a sum of two positive impedances and is always positive, so the sign of the coefficient is the sign of the downward impedance change. A drop in impedance downward, such as shale onto a porous gas sand, gives a negative coefficient and a soft event.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-reflection-coefficients', 10, 'One interface has a reflection coefficient of -0.017688 and another has +0.0130. Which is the stronger reflection?', '["The +0.0130 interface, because positive coefficients return more energy","Neither, because coefficients of opposite sign cannot be compared","The -0.017688 interface, because 0.017688 is the larger magnitude","The +0.0130 interface, because -0.017688 is the smaller signed value"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Strength is magnitude, so events are ranked by absolute value regardless of sign. 0.017688 exceeds 0.0130, so the negative interface returns more energy; it simply returns it inverted. This is exactly why the capstone grades 0.017688 rather than -0.017688.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-reflection-coefficients', 11, 'A shale of impedance 7000 overlies a tight carbonate of impedance 11000. What is the reflection coefficient, and what kind of event is it?', '["+4000, a hard event","-0.2222, a soft event","+0.4444, a hard event","+0.2222, a hard event"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The numerator is 11000 minus 7000, which is 4000, and the denominator is 11000 plus 7000, which is 18000, giving +0.2222. Impedance increases downward, so it is a hard event. The option +4000 is unnormalised, -0.2222 reverses the layer order, and +0.4444 divides by 9000, the average of the two impedances, rather than by their sum.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-reflection-coefficients', 12, 'Why can you not say from a positive reflection coefficient alone whether an event will appear as a peak on a given seismic section?', '["Because positive coefficients paint as peaks only above about 0.1 in magnitude","Because recording, processing and display polarity conventions differ between surveys and regions","Because the sign of the coefficient changes with the depth of the interface","Because peaks and troughs are assigned at random by interpretation software"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The sign of the coefficient is a property of the earth and never changes, but how amplitude is recorded, whether processing flipped the volume, and which direction and colour the display assigns are all conventions. Establish and state the convention for every volume, because a tie without a stated convention is not reproducible.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-reflection-coefficients', 13, 'Where does the strongest reflection coefficient on the teaching well sit?', '["At 1582 ms, which is 1582 m under the teaching time to depth function","At 1580 ms, which is 1580 m under the teaching time to depth function","At 1586 ms, at the deep end of the neighbourhood window","At 1500 ms, where the impedance log begins"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The strongest coefficient, 0.017688 in magnitude and negative in sign, is at 1582 ms. The teaching time to depth function is set up so that milliseconds and metres coincide on this well, so the same event is at 1582 m.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-reflection-coefficients', 14, 'The coefficients at 1578, 1580, 1582, 1584 and 1586 ms are -0.017366, -0.017629, -0.017688, -0.017538 and -0.017238. What does that pattern tell you?', '["The peak is a noise spike, because five consecutive samples share the same sign","The impedance log needs despiking before the series can be trusted","The peak is a genuine event, because the values rise and fall smoothly around a single crest","The reflection at 1582 ms should be discarded in favour of the one at 1586 ms"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Every value in the 8 ms window is negative and close to the peak; the largest magnitude exceeds the smallest by only 0.000450, about 2.5 percent of the peak, and the step from 1580 to 1582 is just 0.000059. A noise spike would show one outlying value with neighbours near the background level, often differing in sign.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-reflection-coefficients', 15, 'One of the two impedance samples at an interface is missing. What does the engine put in the reflectivity series there?', '["Zero, so the trace stays continuous through the missing interval","A no-data value, because zero would falsely claim that nothing reflects there","The coefficient from the nearest valid interface above","The average of the coefficients on either side of the hole"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A gap produces a gap, never a zero. Zero is a real coefficient meaning the two impedances match and nothing reflects, whereas no-data means the answer is unknown. Filling holes with zeros would manufacture a claim of acoustic transparency that would then be convolved with the wavelet and displayed as if it had been measured.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-wavelets-and-convolution', 1, 'In the convolutional model, what is the wavelet?', '["The series of spikes produced by impedance contrasts in the earth","The two way time axis on which the trace is plotted","The short band limited oscillating pulse the source emits, a scaled copy of which appears at every reflection","The filter applied during processing to remove multiples"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A seismic source cannot deliver energy in an instant, so it emits a short oscillating pulse of finite bandwidth. Every reflection in the recorded trace is a smeared copy of that pulse, scaled by the reflection coefficient. The spike series is the earth''s contribution, the wavelet is the acquisition''s.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-wavelets-and-convolution', 2, 'What is the shape of a Ricker wavelet?', '["A single positive hump with no negative excursions","A steadily decaying oscillation with many lobes of falling size","Two equal peaks separated by a central trough","A single central peak with two negative side lobes, symmetric about the centre"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The exponential factor makes the pulse short, the bracket in front makes it swing negative on both sides. The result is one central peak flanked by two negative side lobes that are equal to each other by symmetry.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-wavelets-and-convolution', 3, 'What is the amplitude of the engine''s Ricker wavelet at 0 ms?', '["It depends on the dominant frequency, rising with higher frequency","1.0 at every dominant frequency, because the wavelet is peak normalised","0.4449 at 25 Hz and 0.4463 at 15 Hz","It depends on the largest reflection coefficient in the well"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Substituting t equal to zero gives a bracket of 1 and an exponential of 1, so the centre sample reads 1.0 for any frequency. The wavelet therefore carries pure shape, and all physical scale in the synthetic comes from the reflection coefficients.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-wavelets-and-convolution', 4, 'Sampled at 2 ms with a 60 ms half length, how long is the wavelet the engine builds?', '["30 samples spanning 0 to 60 ms","60 samples spanning minus 30 to plus 30 ms","61 samples spanning minus 60 ms to plus 60 ms","120 samples spanning minus 120 to plus 120 ms"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Two times 60 divided by 2, plus one, gives 61 samples running from minus 60 ms to plus 60 ms, a total length of 120 ms. The count is odd so the wavelet has a true centre sample, which is treated as zero lag during convolution.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-wavelets-and-convolution', 5, 'What is the side lobe minimum of the engine''s Ricker at 25 Hz?', '["About minus 0.4449","About minus 0.4463","About minus 1.0","About minus 0.0177"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine reports about minus 0.4449 at 25 Hz and at 40 Hz, and about minus 0.4463 at 15 Hz. Both negative side lobes of a given wavelet are equal, so one number describes both.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-wavelets-and-convolution', 6, 'Why does the side lobe read minus 0.4463 at 15 Hz but minus 0.4449 at 25 Hz and 40 Hz?', '["Lower frequencies genuinely have deeper side lobes than higher ones","The engine normalises 15 Hz wavelets differently from higher frequency ones","The side lobes are affected by the reflection coefficients in the well","Sampling: the continuous minimum is minus 0.4463 at any frequency, and the 2 ms grid happens to land almost on it at 15 Hz but between samples at 25 and 40 Hz"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The continuous Ricker has a minimum of minus two times exp of minus 1.5, which works out to minus 0.4463 regardless of frequency. The engine only sees samples every 2 ms, so at 25 Hz and 40 Hz the nearest sample sits slightly above the true trough.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-wavelets-and-convolution', 7, 'Why does zero phase matter for interpretation?', '["It guarantees the synthetic and the seismic have the same amplitude scale","The wavelet is symmetric with its peak at the centre, so the event sits on the boundary rather than beside it","It removes the side lobes from the wavelet","It makes the wavelet independent of the sample rate"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Because time appears only as t squared, the Ricker is symmetric about time zero with its maximum at the centre. Placed at a reflection coefficient, its peak lands at the same time as that coefficient, so picking the peak picks the interface.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-wavelets-and-convolution', 8, 'Which description of convolution matches what the engine does?', '["Multiply the reflectivity series by the wavelet sample by sample","Replace each wavelet sample with the nearest reflection coefficient","Place a copy of the wavelet at every reflection coefficient, scaled by that coefficient, then add all the copies sample by sample","Average the reflectivity series over a window as long as the wavelet"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The operational recipe is exact rather than approximate: scale a wavelet copy by each coefficient, centre it on that coefficient''s time, and sum every copy on the common time grid. The result is the synthetic trace.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-wavelets-and-convolution', 9, 'A reflection coefficient of minus 0.02 sits at 1600 ms with no neighbours within 200 ms. Using a 25 Hz Ricker, what does the trace show at 1600 ms?', '["A trough of about minus 0.02 with positive side lobes on either side","A peak of about plus 0.02 with negative side lobes on either side","A trough of about minus 0.4449, the side lobe value","Nothing, because negative coefficients are not convolved"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A negative coefficient flips the wavelet copy, turning the central peak into a trough and the two negative side lobes into positive ones. Because the reflection is isolated, no summing occurs, so the peak amplitude equals the coefficient and the peak time equals the interface time.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-wavelets-and-convolution', 10, 'How does the length of the wavelet relate to dominant frequency and resolution?', '["Higher frequency gives a longer wavelet and finer resolution","Higher frequency gives a shorter wavelet and finer resolution, lower frequency gives a longer wavelet that smears","Frequency changes the amplitude of the wavelet but not its length","Resolution is set by the sample rate alone, not by the wavelet"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Frequency and time enter the Ricker only as their product, so doubling the frequency squeezes the same shape into half the time. Two reflections must be separated by roughly the wavelet length before their copies stop overlapping, so a shorter wavelet separates more.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-wavelets-and-convolution', 11, 'The engine gives the strongest synthetic amplitude on the teaching well as 0.157315 at 15 Hz, 0.073005 at 25 Hz and 0.036223 at 40 Hz. What does this pattern show?', '["The lowest frequency gives the largest peak, because its longer wavelet sums more neighbouring reflections constructively","The highest frequency gives the largest peak, as expected from better bandwidth","Amplitude is independent of frequency and the differences are rounding","The 15 Hz result must be a processing error, since higher frequency should always be brighter"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Amplitude falls as frequency rises here, the opposite of the naive expectation. A long wavelet collects contributions from reflections spread over a wide span of time, and where they share a sign they reinforce. Resolution and amplitude are different things.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-wavelets-and-convolution', 12, 'At 25 Hz the strongest synthetic amplitude on the teaching well is 0.073005 at 1642 ms, while the largest reflection coefficient is 0.017688 in absolute value at 1582 ms. What best explains the 60 ms gap?', '["The time to depth function is 60 ms out and the synthetic needs a bulk shift","The strongest coefficient was excluded from the convolution because it is negative","A cluster of moderate coefficients near 1642 ms reinforces constructively and sums to more than the isolated larger coefficient at 1582 ms produces alone","The wavelet peak is offset from its centre by 60 ms"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Every amplitude sample is a sum of overlapping scaled wavelet copies. The coefficient at 1582 ms is the biggest single one but stands relatively alone, so its copy peaks at roughly the coefficient itself. The group near 1642 ms adds up to about four times that.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-wavelets-and-convolution', 13, 'In general, why need the time of the strongest amplitude in a synthetic not coincide with the time of the strongest reflection coefficient?', '["Because the wavelet delays every event by half its length","Because reflection coefficients are computed in depth and amplitudes in time","Because negative coefficients are plotted at the following sample","Because each amplitude sample is a sum of overlapping wavelet copies, so a reinforcing group of moderate coefficients can outproduce one larger isolated coefficient"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Amplitude is never a single reflection''s strength. On the teaching well the strongest coefficient is 0.017688 in absolute value, yet the strongest amplitudes are 0.036223, 0.073005 and 0.157315, so each peak is many copies summing, and the largest sum need not land where the largest single contribution lands.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-wavelets-and-convolution', 14, 'At 15 Hz the strongest amplitude falls at 1580 ms, within one sample of the strongest reflection coefficient at 1582 ms, but at 25 Hz it falls at 1642 ms. What does the comparison show?', '["The 15 Hz run used a different well interval from the 25 Hz run","Only the 15 Hz result is trustworthy, because its peak matches a real interface","Where the trace is brightest depends on the wavelet as well as the earth, since the reflectivity is identical in both runs","The strongest coefficient moved in time when the frequency was changed"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The reflectivity series comes from the logs and the time to depth function and is identical in both runs. Only the wavelet differs, and a longer wavelet gathers a different set of neighbours into each sum, so the brightest part of the trace moves.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-wavelets-and-convolution', 15, 'Which conclusion should an interpreter carry out of this module?', '["A bright amplitude always marks a large impedance contrast","A bright amplitude is not automatically a strong interface, since it can also be constructive summing, tuning, or a long wavelet smearing a package together","Amplitude should be ignored, since only the reflectivity series is meaningful","Destructive interference is harmless because cancelled events reappear at higher frequency"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Brightness can come from one large contrast, from several moderate contrasts adding, from a bed at tuning thickness, or from a low frequency wavelet blending a package. A dim event can equally be a strong contrast cancelled by a neighbour, which is the more dangerous case.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-synthetic', 1, 'The summary panel reports a mean sonic velocity. Which part of the pipeline produces that number?', '["The DT curve alone, before any time conversion has happened","The impedance log, after velocity and density have been multiplied","The reflectivity series, after impedance contrasts have been formed","The convolved trace, after the wavelet has been applied"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The mean sonic velocity is the earliest number on the panel. The app inverts each finite DT sample into a velocity and averages those velocities, with no density read and no depth converted to time. Because it sits first in the chain, it is the cleanest diagnostic on the panel.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-synthetic', 2, 'Where does the panel''s TWT at the top of the log come from?', '["It is picked from the observed seismic trace","It is the time of the first sample of the convolved synthetic","It is the time-depth function applied to the shallowest log sample","It is the midpoint of the valid window"]'::jsonb, 2, 'It is arithmetic on a depth. The time-depth function is applied to the shallowest log sample, which sits at 1500 m, and nothing about the seismic or the wavelet is involved.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-synthetic', 3, 'Reading down the panel in pipeline order, which entry is the first that requires both input curves to be correct?', '["Mean sonic velocity","Maximum impedance","TWT at the top of the log","Strongest synthetic amplitude"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Impedance is the sample by sample product of velocity and density, so it is the first entry fed by both DT and RHOB. The mean sonic velocity uses DT alone and the TWT at the top of the log uses a depth, which is why a bad density shows up at the impedance entry while leaving the velocity untouched.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-synthetic', 4, 'Which statement about the strongest synthetic amplitude on the panel is correct?', '["It is scanned over the whole 0 to 1798 ms grid, with samples outside the log counted as zeros","It is read straight off the reflectivity series","It does not depend on the wavelet frequency","It comes from the convolved trace, and only samples inside the valid window are searched because the rest are marked as gaps"]'::jsonb, 3, 'It is the only panel entry that depends on the wavelet, so the frequency must always be quoted with it, and the search skips gaps so the reported peak comes from a part of the trace the well actually supports.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-synthetic', 5, 'Why does the engine mark samples outside the logged interval as gaps rather than filling them with zeros?', '["Zeros would make the trace longer than the time grid allows","A zero asserts an amplitude nobody measured, while a gap asserts nothing, and the panel statistics skip gaps","Gaps make the convolution faster to compute","Zeros cannot be stored in the array the engine uses"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The log occupies only 1500 to 1650 ms of a grid running 0 to 1798 ms. Zeros are still numbers and get swept into any statistic taken over the full trace, diluting averages toward zero and creating edge artefacts. A gap records the absence of information, which is the truth outside the log.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-synthetic', 6, 'A LAS file carries DT in microseconds per foot and the software assumes microseconds per metre. What happens?', '["Velocities come out about 3.28 times too low and the software raises a unit error","Nothing changes, because impedance is a ratio and the units cancel","Velocities come out about 3.28 times too high, and nothing crashes or warns","Times are halved while the velocities stay correct"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A foot is shorter than a metre, so the per-foot transit time is the smaller number; inverting it as though it were metric gives too fast a velocity, roughly by the ratio of the units. Every curve still plots and the synthetic still looks like a synthetic, which is exactly what makes this failure worth an explicit check against the LAS header.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-synthetic', 7, 'A time-depth conversion that omits the return leg of the ray path produces which symptom on the panel?', '["Every reported time is exactly halved, so the top of the teaching log reads 750 ms instead of 1500 ms","Every reported time doubles, so the top of the log reads 3000 ms","Amplitudes halve while the times stay correct","Impedance halves while the times stay correct"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Seismic time is two-way time, so the function must include both legs. Drop one and every time is halved, while the synthetic remains smooth and well formed and simply sits far too shallow.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-synthetic', 8, 'Why does the QC list insist that polarity is stated rather than assumed?', '["Because the polarity convention changes the impedance values themselves","Because a stated polarity removes the need for any bulk shift later","Because polarity determines the sample rate the synthetic is computed on","Because polarity is a display convention, so two interpreters using opposite conventions tie the same well half a loop apart with nothing in the display to show why"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Polarity is a convention rather than a fact. Recording which sign is used, and which way an increase in impedance is displayed, is what makes two ties comparable.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-synthetic', 9, 'An unhandled null of -999.25 is left in the DT curve. Why is the damage wider than the single bad sample?', '["It is not; arithmetic ignores sentinel values, so only that sample is lost","It shifts the whole time-depth function, so every time on the panel moves","It corrupts that velocity, then the impedance, then the reflection coefficients that sample takes part in, and the wavelet then spreads the error across a band of the synthetic tens of milliseconds wide","It forces the entire trace to be masked as a gap, so nothing can be read from the panel"]'::jsonb, 2, 'To arithmetic a sentinel is just a large negative number, and it propagates down the pipeline. Convolution then smears the corrupted coefficients across the trace, which is why nulls are declared and excluded before anything is computed.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-synthetic', 10, 'An interpreter reports a reflection coefficient close to 1 from a shale and sand section. What is the right reading?', '["An unusually strong but genuine interface that is worth mapping first","An input error in DT or RHOB, because sedimentary coefficients run to a few hundredths","A correct value, since tuning can push a coefficient toward 1","A polarity convention problem that will disappear when the sign is flipped"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A coefficient of 1 is a perfect mirror. Inside a normal sedimentary section coefficients are a few hundredths, and on the teaching well the strongest is 0.017688, so an extraordinary value is a reason to go back and check the curve that produced it.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-synthetic', 11, 'Which set of quantities does the Associate capstone actually grade?', '["Mean sonic velocity, minimum impedance, maximum impedance, the TWT at the base of the log, the strongest reflection coefficient and the strongest synthetic amplitude","Mean sonic velocity, TWT at the top of the log, maximum impedance, the strongest reflection coefficient, the strongest synthetic amplitude and the recovered bulk shift","Mean sonic velocity, TWT at the top of the log, maximum impedance, the strongest reflection coefficient in absolute value, the TWT of that coefficient and the TWT of the strongest synthetic amplitude","Sample count, sample step, mean sonic velocity, maximum impedance, the strongest reflection coefficient and the tuning thickness"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Six numbers are read from the summary panel with the wavelet set to 25 Hz, each checked within a stated tolerance. The bulk shift belongs to the Professional tier and the tuning thickness to the Expert tier, so neither is graded here.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-synthetic', 12, 'Which sequence does the server enforce on the deep path before the capstone will open?', '["Pass the final exam at 75 percent, then the module quizzes at 70 percent, then the capstone","Sit the capstone first, and work back through the module quizzes only if it is failed","Read every lesson, pass each module quiz at 70 percent, then the capstone, with no final exam involved","Read every lesson, pass each module quiz at 75 percent with a 24 hour cooldown after three failures, clear all six modules, pass the final exam at 70 percent, and only then does the capstone unlock"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The capstone is the last gate, and passing it grants the Associate certification for the course. The cooldown after three failed attempts is deliberate, since it sends a learner back to the lessons rather than letting them guess through the bank.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-synthetic', 13, 'A learner reports the same TWT for the strongest reflection coefficient and for the strongest synthetic amplitude. What has been misunderstood?', '["Nothing, because the two always coincide when the wavelet is zero phase","That the trace is a sum of overlapping wavelet copies, so its largest excursion need not sit at the largest single coefficient; on this well the two times are 60 ms apart","That the time grid uses a 2 ms sample step rather than 1 ms","That the two-way factor has to be applied twice, once to each entry"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Each coefficient contributes a scaled and shifted copy of the wavelet, and the copies overlap. The peak of the sum lands where contributions reinforce one another. Frequency also moves it, which is why the capstone fixes the wavelet at 25 Hz.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-synthetic', 14, 'In the Professional tier, why is the observed seismic built by planting a lag that the engine already knows?', '["So the shift recovered by the cross-correlation scan can be checked against a known truth, which shows that a tie is a measurement rather than an eye judgement","So the cross-correlation converges in fewer iterations","Because real seismic never carries a shift relative to a synthetic","Because the capstone grades the recovered lag alongside the other numbers"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The exercise is honest because there is a right answer to recover. Two interpreters running the same scan get the same shift, while two aligning by eye get two answers and no way to arbitrate between them.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-reading-the-synthetic', 15, 'What does the Expert tier add once the synthetic can be aligned?', '["A second sonic curve so the velocity can be averaged for redundancy","An automatic depth conversion of the whole seismic volume","Wedge modelling of a reflection pair with equal and opposite coefficients, to find the tuning thickness where amplitude peaks and to compare it against the classical theoretical value","A null handling pass over the input curves before the impedance is formed"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The progression across the tiers is that the beginner builds the synthetic, the professional aligns it to the seismic, and the expert quantifies what resolution can and cannot see. The wedge model is how that last question is made quantitative.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'final', null, 1, 'A geologist has a well whose formation tops are picked to the nearest metre, and a seismic volume whose vertical axis is two way time. What does the well tie give that neither dataset gives on its own?', '["A conversion of the seismic amplitudes into porosity units","A depth to time relationship, so the precise well depths can be compared directly against the broad seismic reflections","A replacement for the sonic log, so bulk density alone is enough","A way to remove all noise from the seismic volume"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The well is precise but measured in depth; the seismic is broad but measured in two way time. The tie is the bridge between those two axes, which is what lets a well top be placed on a seismic reflection with confidence.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'final', null, 2, 'A thick, uniform sandstone has the same acoustic impedance from top to base. Ignoring noise, what does a seismic trace record from the middle of that sandstone, well away from its top and its base?', '["A strong reflection, because the sand has a high impedance value","A reflection whose amplitude is proportional to the thickness of the sand","Essentially nothing, because seismic responds to contrasts in impedance at interfaces rather than to the value of impedance itself","A reflection whose amplitude equals the impedance divided by 1000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Seismic energy is reflected where impedance changes. Inside a uniform layer there is no change, so there is no interface and no reflection, no matter how high or low the absolute impedance happens to be.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'final', null, 3, 'In the convolutional model this course uses, a synthetic seismic trace is built from which two ingredients?', '["The reflection coefficient series and the wavelet","The gamma ray log and the neutron porosity log","The depth axis and the sample rate","The recorded seismic trace and the well tops"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The convolutional model states that a trace is the reflection coefficient series convolved with a wavelet. The reflectivity comes from the logs, the wavelet stands in for the source pulse, and everything else in the workflow exists to produce those two things.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'final', null, 4, 'The basic_20 teaching well is logged from 1500 m to 1650 m with a 0.5 m sample step, with both ends included. How many log samples is that?', '["150","300","3001","301"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The logged interval is 150 m. At 0.5 m per step that is 300 steps, and counting both the first and the last sample gives 300 + 1 = 301 samples, which matches the 301 finite samples the summary panel reports.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'final', null, 5, 'Which set of curves does basic_20 carry, and which of them does the well tie workflow actually consume?', '["DEPT, GR, RHOB, NPHI and DT; the tie is driven by RHOB and DT","DEPT, GR, RHOB, NPHI and DT; the tie is driven by GR and NPHI","DEPT and DT only; the tie is driven by DEPT and DT","DEPT, GR, RHOB, NPHI and DT; the tie is driven by NPHI and RHOB"]'::jsonb, 0, 'basic_20 carries DEPT, GR, RHOB, NPHI and DT. Impedance is density times velocity, so the tie needs RHOB for density and DT for velocity. GR and NPHI are useful geologically but play no part in building the synthetic.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'final', null, 6, 'Put the core steps of the beginner well tie workflow into the order this course uses.', '["Convolve with a wavelet, then compute reflection coefficients, then convert depth to time, then compute impedance","Compute impedance, then convert depth to time, then compute reflection coefficients, then convolve with a wavelet","Convert depth to time, then convolve with a wavelet, then compute impedance, then compute reflection coefficients","Compute reflection coefficients, then compute impedance, then convert depth to time, then convolve with a wavelet"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Velocity and density give impedance, the time conversion moves the log onto the seismic axis, contrasts in impedance give the reflection coefficient series, and convolution with a wavelet turns that series into a synthetic trace. Each step consumes the output of the one before it.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'final', null, 7, 'Wells and seismic differ in two distinct ways, and the tie has to bridge both. Which statement states that pair of differences correctly?', '["Wells are broad and recorded in time, while seismic is precise and recorded in depth","Both are recorded in depth, and only the sample rate differs","Wells are precise and recorded in depth, while seismic is vertically broad and recorded in two way time","Wells measure impedance directly, while seismic measures bulk density directly"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Two gaps have to be closed at once: a resolution gap, since the log samples centimetres to decimetres while a seismic wavelet smears over tens of metres, and an axis gap, since the log is in depth and the seismic is in two way time.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'final', null, 8, 'A sonic log reads DT = 400 us/m. Using v = 1000000 / DT, what is the interval velocity?', '["0.0025 m/s","250 m/s","400000000 m/s","2500 m/s"]'::jsonb, 3, '1000000 divided by 400 is 2500 m/s. Taking the reciprocal without the factor of 1000000 gives 0.0025, and multiplying instead of dividing gives 400000000, so both of those are the classic slips to avoid.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'final', null, 9, 'At 1580 m the basic_20 well reads DT = 305.142 us/m. What is the interval velocity there, to the nearest m/s?', '["3277 m/s","305142 m/s","0.0033 m/s","2502 m/s"]'::jsonb, 0, 'v = 1000000 / 305.142 = 3277.16, which rounds to 3277 m/s. The value 2502 m/s is the velocity at the top of the log, where DT is 399.737 us/m, so it belongs to a different depth.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'final', null, 10, 'A layer has an interval velocity of 3200 m/s and a bulk density of 2.45. Using the impedance convention in this course, Z = rho times v, what is its acoustic impedance?', '["1306","7840","3200","784"]'::jsonb, 1, '3200 times 2.45 is 7840. Dividing velocity by density instead of multiplying gives 1306, and treating impedance as equal to velocity gives 3200, so neither of those reflects the definition.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'final', null, 11, 'At 1582 m, basic_20 reads DT = 313.949 us/m and RHOB = 2.4181. What is the acoustic impedance at that depth, to the nearest whole unit?', '["3185","759","7702","1317"]'::jsonb, 2, 'First convert the sonic reading: v = 1000000 / 313.949 = 3185.23 m/s. Then multiply by density: 3185.23 times 2.4181 = 7702.2. The value 759 comes from multiplying DT by density without converting to velocity first, and 3185 stops at the velocity step.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'final', null, 12, 'Why does the workflow build a full impedance log before it does anything about reflections?', '["Because impedance is the quantity the sonic tool measures directly","Because an impedance log removes the need for a wavelet later","Because impedance is already expressed in two way time","Because reflections are produced by contrasts in acoustic impedance, so the impedance log is exactly the input the reflection calculation needs"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Reflection strength depends on how impedance changes across an interface, so the impedance curve is the raw material for the reflection coefficient series. The sonic tool measures transit time, not impedance, and the wavelet is still required afterwards.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'final', null, 13, 'At 1600 m, basic_20 reads DT = 383.856 us/m with RHOB = 2.2593. At 1650 m it reads DT = 277.473 us/m with RHOB = 2.2724. Which depth has the higher acoustic impedance, and by roughly how much?', '["1650 m, by about 2300","1600 m, by about 2300","1600 m, by about 106, which is the difference in the sonic readings","1650 m, by about 0.013, which is the difference in the densities"]'::jsonb, 0, 'At 1600 m, v = 2605.14 and Z = 5885.8. At 1650 m, v = 3603.95 and Z = 8189.63. The difference is about 2304 in favour of 1650 m. The densities are almost identical, so nearly all of the impedance change comes from the sonic log.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'final', null, 14, 'Across the 301 finite samples of basic_20 the impedance runs from about 5436 at its lowest to about 10625 at its highest. What does that spread tell you before you compute anything further?', '["That the well contains no reflecting interfaces","That the section holds real contrasts in rock properties, so a non trivial reflection coefficient series should be expected","That the sonic log must be in us/ft rather than us/m","That every impedance value has to be halved before use"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The maximum is close to twice the minimum, so impedance genuinely varies through the logged interval. Somewhere in that interval there are interfaces with non zero reflection coefficients, which is what makes a synthetic worth computing here.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'final', null, 15, 'A reflector sits 1200 m below the surface and the average velocity of everything above it is 2400 m/s. What is the two way time to that reflector?', '["500 ms","1200 ms","1000 ms","2400 ms"]'::jsonb, 2, 'TWT = 2z / v = 2 times 1200 divided by 2400 = 1.0 s, or 1000 ms. The value 500 ms is the one way travel time, which is what you get if you forget that the energy has to travel down and back again.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'final', null, 16, 'This course uses a teaching overburden velocity of 2000 m/s. Why does that particular number make the depth to time arithmetic easy to check by eye?', '["Because it makes two way time in seconds numerically equal to depth in metres","Because it removes the factor of two from TWT = 2z / v","Because it makes velocity numerically equal to density","Because TWT = 2z / 2000 s means two way time in milliseconds is numerically equal to depth in metres"]'::jsonb, 3, 'With v = 2000 m/s, TWT in seconds is 2z / 2000 = z / 1000, so a depth of 1500 m becomes 1.5 s, which is 1500 ms. The factor of two is still there; it simply cancels neatly against the chosen velocity.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'final', null, 17, 'Using the 2000 m/s teaching overburden, what two way time does the top of the basic_20 log at 1500 m map to?', '["1500 ms","750 ms","3000 ms","0.75 s"]'::jsonb, 0, 'TWT = 2 times 1500 divided by 2000 = 1.5 s, which is 1500 ms. The answers 750 ms and 0.75 s are the one way time, and 3000 ms is what you get if you apply the factor of two but forget to divide by the velocity properly.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'final', null, 18, 'A reflection arrives at 1400 ms two way time and the average velocity above it is 2000 m/s. How deep is the reflector?', '["2800 m","1400 m","700 m","3500 m"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Rearranging TWT = 2z / v gives z = v times TWT / 2 = 2000 times 1.4 / 2 = 1400 m. Forgetting to halve gives 2800 m, and halving twice gives 700 m.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'final', null, 19, 'The synthetic is computed on a regular 2 ms grid of 900 samples starting at 0 ms. What is the time of the last sample?', '["1800 ms","1802 ms","1798 ms","900 ms"]'::jsonb, 2, 'With the first sample at 0 ms there are 899 steps of 2 ms after it, so the last sample sits at 899 times 2 = 1798 ms. Multiplying 900 by 2 gives 1800 ms, which counts one step too many.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'final', null, 20, 'Why does the workflow resample the well curves onto a time grid instead of leaving everything in depth?', '["Because logs are recorded in time and depth has to be derived from them","Because a 0.5 m log step is too coarse to be useful","Because bulk density cannot be plotted against depth","Because the seismic data the synthetic has to be compared against lives on a two way time axis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The whole point of the synthetic is to be laid alongside a real seismic trace, and seismic is recorded in two way time. Logs are recorded in depth, and the 0.5 m step is far finer than seismic needs, not coarser.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'final', null, 21, 'Using the 2000 m/s teaching overburden, the basic_20 log spans 1500 m to 1650 m. Which statement about the resulting time span and the 0 to 1798 ms grid is correct?', '["The log occupies 1500 ms to 1650 ms, so it sits inside the grid with empty time above and below it","The log occupies 750 ms to 825 ms, so it sits in the lower half of the grid","The log occupies 3000 ms to 3300 ms, so it falls off the end of the grid","The log occupies 0 ms to 150 ms, because time is always measured from the top of the log"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The teaching function makes milliseconds equal metres, so the log runs from 1500 ms to 1650 ms. The grid runs from 0 ms to 1798 ms, so the logged interval sits comfortably inside it with unlogged time above it and a short tail below it.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'final', null, 22, 'Layer 1 has an acoustic impedance of 6000, and the layer directly below it has an impedance of 6600. What is the reflection coefficient at that interface?', '["600","+0.048","+0.10","-0.048"]'::jsonb, 1, 'RC = (Z2 - Z1) / (Z2 + Z1) = 600 / 12600 = +0.048. The value 600 is the raw impedance difference with no normalisation, and +0.10 comes from dividing the difference by only the upper impedance instead of by the sum.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'final', null, 23, 'On basic_20 the impedance just above one interface is 7979.58 and just below it is 7702.20. What is the reflection coefficient there, to five decimal places?', '["+0.01769","-277.38","-0.01769","-0.03476"]'::jsonb, 2, 'RC = (7702.20 - 7979.58) / (7702.20 + 7979.58) = -277.38 / 15681.78 = -0.01769. It is negative because impedance drops downwards. Dividing by only the upper impedance gives -0.03476, and leaving out the normalisation entirely gives -277.38.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'final', null, 24, 'Which statement about reflection coefficients is correct?', '["They carry the same units as acoustic impedance","They can exceed +1 wherever the impedance contrast is strong enough","They are always positive, because a seismic amplitude cannot be negative","They are dimensionless, they are bounded between -1 and +1, and on real logs they are usually small"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A reflection coefficient is a ratio of an impedance difference to an impedance sum, so the units cancel and the value can never leave the range -1 to +1. On basic_20 the largest is only about 0.0177, which is typical of a well behaved clastic section.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'final', null, 25, 'A reflection coefficient at an interface comes out negative. What does that mean physically?', '["The layer below has lower acoustic impedance than the layer above, so the reflected pulse returns with reversed polarity","The interface lies above the top of the logged interval","The velocity was computed with the wrong sign in v = 1000000 / DT","The two impedances on either side are equal"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The sign of the reflection coefficient follows the sign of Z2 minus Z1. Negative means a downward step into softer rock, and the wavelet copy placed at that interface is flipped, so a zero phase wavelet produces a trough instead of a peak.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'final', null, 26, 'On basic_20 the largest reflection coefficient by absolute value is about 0.0177. What is its sign, and at what two way time does it sit?', '["Positive, at 1642 ms","Negative, at 1582 ms","Positive, at 1582 ms","Negative, at 1642 ms"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The strongest coefficient is -0.017688 at 1582 ms, which is the same interface as the 7979.58 to 7702.20 impedance pair. It is a drop in impedance, so it is negative. The time 1642 ms belongs to the strongest synthetic amplitude at 25 Hz, which is a different thing entirely.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'final', null, 27, 'Around the strongest reflection coefficient on basic_20, the neighbouring values at 1578, 1580, 1584 and 1586 ms are -0.01737, -0.01763, -0.01754 and -0.01724. What does that pattern tell you about the interface at 1582 ms?', '["It is an isolated spike, with coefficients of zero on either side of it","The coefficients alternate in sign, which is the signature of a tuning wedge","It is the peak of a run of similar negative coefficients, so the impedance drop is spread over several samples rather than being one sharp step","The sign reverses just below 1582 ms, so polarity flips at that interface"]'::jsonb, 2, 'All five values are negative and close in size, so the impedance is falling steadily across roughly ten milliseconds rather than stepping down at a single sample. That is why the synthetic response there is broader than a single wavelet copy would be.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'final', null, 28, 'Two adjacent samples on the impedance log happen to hold exactly the same value. What reflection coefficient does that interface produce, and what does the synthetic show there?', '["RC = +1, and the synthetic shows a full amplitude peak","RC = -1, and the synthetic shows a full amplitude trough","RC is undefined, and the sample has to be discarded","RC = 0, and that interface contributes nothing at all to the synthetic"]'::jsonb, 3, 'With Z2 equal to Z1 the numerator (Z2 - Z1) is zero, so the reflection coefficient is zero. Convolution places a wavelet copy scaled by zero at that time, which adds nothing. The formula is only undefined if both impedances were zero, which does not happen on real logs.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'final', null, 29, 'In the convolutional model, what does the wavelet represent?', '["The shape of the pulse the seismic source sends into the ground, a scaled copy of which comes back from every reflector","The reflection coefficient series after it has been normalised","The depth to time conversion function","The bulk density curve resampled onto the 2 ms grid"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A real source does not emit an instantaneous spike, it emits a short pulse with a finite length. Every reflector returns a copy of that pulse, scaled by its reflection coefficient, which is exactly what convolution encodes.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'final', null, 30, 'The zero phase Ricker wavelet used in this course has 61 samples at a 2 ms step. Over what time range is it defined, and what is its peak value?', '["0 to 120 ms, with a peak of 0.5","-60 to +60 ms, with a peak of 1.0 at 0 ms","-30 to +30 ms, with a peak of 1.0 at 0 ms","-60 to +60 ms, with a peak of -0.445 at 0 ms"]'::jsonb, 1, '61 samples at 2 ms spans 120 ms, and because the wavelet is zero phase it is centred on zero, so it runs from -60 ms to +60 ms with its peak of 1.0 at 0 ms. The value -0.445 is the side lobe minimum, not the peak.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'final', null, 31, 'A single reflection coefficient of -0.02 is convolved with the Ricker wavelet, whose central peak is 1.0 and whose side lobe minimum is about -0.445. What does the response from that one reflector look like?', '["-0.02 at the centre, with -0.0089 on the side lobes","+0.02 at the centre, with -0.0089 on the side lobes","-0.02 at the centre, with +0.0089 on the side lobes","-0.445 at the centre, with -0.02 on the side lobes"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Convolution scales the whole wavelet by the coefficient. The centre becomes -0.02 times 1.0 = -0.02, and each side lobe becomes -0.02 times -0.445 = +0.0089. Note that the side lobes flip sign along with the peak, which is why a single reflector still spreads energy across several samples in both directions.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'final', null, 32, 'How does this course describe convolution?', '["Multiplying the wavelet by the average impedance of the interval","Taking the difference between neighbouring reflection coefficients","Stretching one wavelet so that it spans the whole logged interval","Placing a copy of the wavelet at every reflection coefficient, scaling each copy by that coefficient, and summing all the copies"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Convolution is a scale and sum operation. Each reflection coefficient contributes one scaled, possibly flipped copy of the wavelet at its own time, and the synthetic trace at any sample is the total of every copy that reaches that sample.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'final', null, 33, 'On basic_20 the strongest synthetic amplitude is 0.157 at 15 Hz, 0.073 at 25 Hz and 0.036 at 40 Hz. Why does the lowest frequency give the largest peak on this well?', '["Because the long 15 Hz wavelet reaches across several closely spaced coefficients of the same sign and sums them constructively, while the shorter high frequency wavelet keeps them separate","Because a low frequency Ricker wavelet has a larger peak value than a high frequency one","Because the reflection coefficients themselves become larger when the frequency is lowered","Because 15 Hz shifts every reflector to a shallower two way time"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Every Ricker used here peaks at 1.0 and the reflectivity never changes with frequency, so the difference is pure interference. basic_20 has runs of similar sign coefficients, and the broad 15 Hz wavelet adds them together. Do not assume higher frequency means bigger amplitude; on this well it is the reverse.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'final', null, 34, 'Two interfaces sit closer together in time than the length of the wavelet. What appears on the synthetic?', '["Two clearly separated events, each at its own correct time","The two wavelet copies overlap and interfere, giving one composite event whose amplitude and timing match neither interface on its own","The shallower of the two interfaces is dropped from the calculation","An amplitude equal to the sum of the two impedances"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This is tuning. Once the separation falls below the wavelet length the copies cannot be told apart, and the peak you see is a blend. Its amplitude may be larger or smaller than either reflector alone, depending on the signs, and its time can sit between the two.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'final', null, 35, 'What is the trade off when the wavelet frequency is raised from 15 Hz to 40 Hz on this well?', '["Resolution falls but amplitudes rise","Both resolution and amplitude rise together","Resolution improves, because the shorter wavelet separates closely spaced reflectors, but each event carries less summed amplitude","Nothing changes, because the reflection coefficients are the same either way"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A higher frequency wavelet is shorter, so it resolves thinner beds, but it also gathers less constructive contribution from neighbouring reflectors. On basic_20 that shows up directly: the strongest amplitude falls from 0.157 at 15 Hz to 0.036 at 40 Hz even though the reflectivity never changed.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'final', null, 36, 'Which six quantities does the capstone grade?', '["Mean gamma ray, mean neutron porosity, mean density, mean sonic, sample count and log step","Wavelet frequency, wavelet length, side lobe value, peak value, sample rate and grid length","Mean velocity, mean density, mean impedance, mean reflection coefficient, mean amplitude and sample count","Mean sonic velocity, two way time at the log top, maximum impedance, the strongest reflection coefficient by absolute value, the two way time of that coefficient, and the two way time of the strongest synthetic amplitude"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The six graded numbers walk through the whole workflow in order: velocity from the sonic, the depth to time step, impedance, reflectivity, where the strongest reflectivity sits, and where the strongest synthetic amplitude sits. Each one is checked against its own tolerance.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'final', null, 37, 'The capstone asks for the mean sonic velocity of basic_20, graded to a tolerance of 1 m/s. Which value is correct?', '["3145.29 m/s","2501.65 m/s","3603.95 m/s","317.94 m/s"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Averaging the velocity over all 301 finite samples gives 3145.29 m/s. The values 2501.65 and 3603.95 are single sample velocities, at 1500 m and at 1650 m, and 317.94 is a transit time in us/m rather than a velocity.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'final', null, 38, 'The summary panel for basic_20 reports a maximum impedance of 10624.96 and a minimum of 5436.47. If those two values happened to sit on either side of a single interface, with the high value in the layer above, what reflection coefficient would that interface give?', '["-5188.49","-0.323","+0.323","-0.489"]'::jsonb, 1, 'RC = (5436.47 - 10624.96) / (5436.47 + 10624.96) = -5188.49 / 16061.43 = -0.323. It is negative because the impedance drops downwards, and -5188.49 is the unnormalised difference. Note how much larger this hypothetical contrast is than the real strongest coefficient of 0.0177, because the actual maximum and minimum are far apart in the well, not adjacent.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'final', null, 39, 'The basic_20 LAS file uses -999.25 as its NULL value. What has to happen to those entries before a mean velocity or an impedance range is computed?', '["They should be replaced with zero so the arithmetic still runs","They should be converted with v = 1000000 / DT like every other sample","They should be excluded, so that all statistics are taken over finite samples only","They should be used to mark the top and the base of the logged interval"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A NULL is an absence of measurement, not a measurement of an unusual value. Feeding -999.25 into the velocity or impedance formulas would poison every summary number, which is why the panel reports statistics over finite samples and states the count it used.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'final', null, 40, 'In the 25 Hz capstone the strongest reflection coefficient sits at 1582 ms, but the strongest synthetic amplitude sits at 1642 ms. Which explanation is correct?', '["The synthetic was accidentally shifted by 60 ms during the depth to time conversion","The reflection coefficient at 1642 ms must be larger than the one at 1582 ms","One of the two numbers is a reporting error, which the capstone tolerance absorbs","The synthetic amplitude at any time is the sum of wavelet copies from every nearby reflector, so a cluster of moderate coefficients near 1642 ms can add up to more than the single strongest coefficient near 1582 ms"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Reflectivity and amplitude are different quantities. Reflectivity is a property of one interface, while amplitude is the summed interference of everything within a wavelet length. The 60 ms gap here is a real feature of the well, not a lag; the planted 8 ms lag belongs to the Professional preview and is a separate idea.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'final', null, 41, 'The Professional tier preview plants a known 8 ms lag between the synthetic and the seismic. What is that exercise teaching?', '["That a tie can be systematically early or late, so the shift has to be measured and applied rather than assumed to be zero","That the wavelet frequency must always be raised to 40 Hz before tying","That the sonic log should be discarded whenever a lag is present","That the time grid must be resampled from 2 ms to 8 ms"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Real ties rarely line up on the first attempt, because the checkshot, the datum or the phase of the data can put the synthetic slightly out. The planted lag gives a known answer to recover, so you can practise measuring a shift instead of guessing at it.'),
  ('seismolord', 'beginner', 'final', null, 42, 'What are the pass rules for this course?', '["Module quizzes at 70 percent, final exam at 75 percent, and the capstone can be taken at any point","Module quizzes at 75 percent with a 24 hour cooldown after three failed attempts, the final exam at 70 percent, and the capstone last","Module quizzes at 75 percent with no cooldown, the final exam at 75 percent, and the capstone first","There are no thresholds, since every activity is advisory"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Each module quiz needs 75 percent, and three failed attempts trigger a 24 hour cooldown before you can try again. The final exam needs 70 percent, and the capstone comes last, once every step of the workflow has been covered.');
